.TH MONOCA 8
.SH NAME
monoca \- publish the authorised identity set as a signed Merkle root
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monoca
[
.B \-k
.I keyfile
]
.B enroll
.I name
.RI [ pkhex
.RI [ exp ]]
.br
.B monoca
[
.B \-k
.I keyfile
]
.B revoke
.I name
.br
.B monoca
[
.B \-k
.I keyfile
]
.B publish
.br
.B monoca
.B epoch
.br
.B monoca
.B root
.br
.B monoca
.B status
.br
.B monoca
[
.B \-k
.I keyfile
]
.B daemon
[
.B \-i
.I seconds
]
.br
.B monoca
.B stop
.br
.B monoca
.B serve
.RI [ addr
.RI [ dir ]]
.br
.B monoca
.B sync
.I addr
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B monoca
maintains the set of identities a fleet trusts.  Each identity is a
leaf
.IP
.EX
blake2b("mono1\e0" || name || "\e0" || pk || exp)
.EE
.IP
where
.I pk
is the 32-byte EdDSA public key in hex and
.I exp
is a Unix time after which the identity is not valid (0 means never).
The leaves are sorted by hash and folded into a Merkle root, so a
holder of one leaf can prove membership with an inclusion path, and a
verifier can prove a key absent with its two neighbouring leaves.  No
sparse tree and no per-user signature are needed.
.PP
Each change re-sorts the leaves, computes the root and signs an epoch
record:
.IP
.EX
epoch   \fIn\fP
time    \fIunix seconds\fP
window  \fIseconds\fP
root    \fI64 hex chars\fP
sig     \fI128 hex chars\fP
.EE
.IP
The signature is EdDSA over
.IP
.EX
"epoch1" || \fIn\fP || \fItime\fP || \fIwindow\fP || root
.EE
.IP
with each integer 8 bytes big-endian, so an epoch signature cannot be
confused with an identity signature.
.SS Commands
.B enroll
adds a leaf.  Re-enrolling a name replaces its previous leaf, so key
rotation removes the old key from the authorised set rather than
leaving both valid.  When
.I pkhex
is omitted the public key is taken from bytes 32 to 64 of the
.B sk
line in
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/mono\fR,
which is what
.IR certify (8)
writes, and
.I exp
defaults to 0 (never expires).
.B revoke
removes every leaf for
.IR name .
Both write the leaves and publish a new epoch under a writer lock.
.PP
.B publish
re-signs the current leaves without changing them.  It is what
.IR certify (8)
runs after a signer rotation.
.B epoch
prints the signed record and
.B root
prints the current root in hex.
.B status
prints the epoch number, time, age, window, freshness, leaf count and
every trusted signer key.
.SS Daemon
.B daemon
publishes when the leaves change or when half the epoch window has
passed, so an unattended CA does not silently age out and reduce every
identity to the
.B box/
scope.  It writes its pid to
.BR /keydb/monoca/daemon ,
refuses to start when another daemon is alive, and sleeps
.I seconds
(default 5) between checks.
.B stop
kills it through
.BR /prog/\fIpid\fP/ctl .
.SS Distribution
.B serve
exports the CA directory with
.IR listen (1)
and
.IR export (1)
on
.I addr
(default
.BR tcp!*!2020 )
from
.I dir
(default
.BR /keydb/monoca ).
The export is plain 9P: the epoch is signed and the root covers the
leaves, so the transport does not have to be trusted, only available.
.B sync
mounts a peer's export at
.BR /n/monoca ,
checks the epoch signature against every key in
.BR /keydb/mono-signer.pub ,
recomputes the Merkle root from the leaves, and refuses a stale epoch
or one older than the local epoch.  Only then does it install
.BR leaves ,
.B epoch
and
.B root
under the writer lock; a failed check leaves the current state alone.
It cannot install an epoch signed by a key this host does not trust.
.IP
.EX
; monoca serve tcp!*!2020                 # on the CA
; monoca sync tcp!ca.example!2020         # on a peer
.EE
.IP
The daemon notices that the installed leaves already match the signed
root and does not republish them, so a sync does not cause an epoch
churn.
.PP
Enroll, revoke, publish and the daemon all take
.BR /keydb/monoca/lock
with
.BR OEXCL ,
so there is one writer at a time; a lock left by a dead process is
removed by checking
.BR /prog/\fIpid\fP .
.IR Svcd (8)
runs it as the
.B monoca
service.  The daemon waits for
.B /keydb/mono-signer
to become readable, so it can start before
.IR certify (8)
has provisioned the host.
.SS Signer
The epoch is signed with
.BR /keydb/mono-signer .
That file is encrypted at rest when a key-encryption key is available:
.B \-k
.IR keyfile ,
or
.B /n/host/keydb/mono.key
when the host supplies one.  The format and the key derivation are in
.IR monosigner (2).
The public half is
.BR /keydb/mono-signer.pub ,
which is all a verifier needs; it may hold several keys during a
rotation.  The daemon decrypts the secret once at start, so the
plaintext key exists only in its memory.
.PP
The record being replaced is retained as
.BI roots/ n\fR,
so a token minted in the previous epoch can still be checked during the
grace window by
.IR mcheck (1).
A verifier accepts a proof only while
.IR time\ +\ window
has not passed; without a verifiable epoch it fails closed rather than
accepting a stale root.
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/monoca/roots/\fIn\fP
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/leaves
authorised set, one
.I "name pkhex exp"
per line
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/epoch
current signed record
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/root
current root, for convenience
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/roots/\fIn\fP
retained previous records
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/lock
writer lock
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca/daemon
pid of the running daemon
.TP
.B /n/monoca
mount point used while
.B sync
reads a peer
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
EdDSA secret key, encrypted when a key file is available
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer.pub
trusted public keys
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/monoca.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mcheck (1),
.IR mono (2),
.IR monosigner (2),
.IR monoauth (8),
.IR certify (8)
.SH BUGS
Distribution is pull-only and manual: a peer runs
.B sync
when told, and the
.B roots
directory is not mirrored, so
.I mcheck
cannot verify an epoch the peer did not see before.  The export has no
access control; anyone who can reach the port can read the public
leaves and the signed epoch.  Serving a snapshot with
.B serve
.I dir
is intended for testing and for a read-only replica.
.PP
An epoch is a full tree rebuild, so enrolling many identities is linear
in the set size; that is adequate for a fleet and is the first thing to
replace with an incremental tree if it stops being so.
.PP
An epoch is checked against the local clock, so a machine whose clock
is not disciplined
.RB ( timed (8))
will treat epochs as stale or not yet valid.
